<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 83 0 R/T(GLP-1 and Weight Management)/E(GLP-1 and Weight Management)/Pg 18 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

320 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 83 0 R/E(GLP-1 plays a central role in appetite regulation by increasing feelings of fullness after meals. When satiety signals are stronger, it becomes easier to manage portion sizes without relying on constant willpower. Over time, improved appetite regulation can support more sustainable weight management.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

321 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 83 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[1559 0 R 0]/K[2]>>
endobj

322 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 83 0 R/T(GLP-1 and Blood Sugar Stability)/E(GLP-1 and Blood Sugar Stability)/Pg 18 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

323 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 83 0 R/E(GLP-1 helps regulate blood sugar by stimulating insulin release when glucose levels rise after eating. It also slows gastric emptying, allowing for a steadier absorption of nutrients into the bloodstream. This combination supports more stable energy levels and fewer post-meal crashes.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

324 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 83 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[1560 0 R 0]/K[5]>>
endobj

325 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 83 0 R/T(GLP-1 and Cravings Control)/E(GLP-1 and Cravings Control)/Pg 18 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

326 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 83 0 R/E(Balanced GLP-1 signaling contributes to more consistent hunger patterns throughout the day. When blood sugar remains stable, cravings for quick carbohydrates and sugary foods often decrease naturally. This helps reduce impulsive eating and supports better food choices overall.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

327 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 83 0 R/Pg 18 0 R/A[1561 0 R 0]/K[8]>>
endobj

