endobj

341 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 84 0 R/E(Protein is one of the strongest dietary triggers for GLP-1 release. Consuming adequate protein at meals enhances satiety signals and supports more stable post-meal blood sugar levels. Including high-quality protein sources consistently can strengthen the body\220s natural appetite regulation system.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

342 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 84 0 R/T(Fiber & Gut Hormones)/E(Fiber & Gut Hormones)/Pg 19 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

343 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 84 0 R/E(Dietary fiber plays a critical role in gut hormone signaling, including GLP-1 production. When fiber is fermented in the colon, it produces short-chain fatty acids that help stimulate GLP-1 release. Higher fiber intake is associated with improved satiety, better glycemic control, and more consistent hunger patterns.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

344 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 84 0 R/T(Healthy Fats and Satiety)/E(Healthy Fats and Satiety)/Pg 19 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

345 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 84 0 R/E(Healthy fats slow digestion and extend feelings of fullness. When paired with protein and fiber, they support a stronger satiety response. Balanced fat intake also contributes to overall metabolic stability.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

346 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 84 0 R/T(Fermented Foods & the Microbiome)/E(Fermented Foods & the Microbiome)/Pg 19 0 R/K[7]>>
