<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 103 0 R/T(Key Lifestyle Influencers of GLP-1)/E(Key Lifestyle Influencers of GLP-1)/Pg 38 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

1245 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1921 0 R/E(Sleep Quality \205 Inadequate sleep disrupts appetite hormones and increases cravings.)/Pg 38 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

1246 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1922 0 R/E(Stress Levels \205 Chronic stress may blunt satiety signaling and increase emotional eating.)/Pg 38 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

1247 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1923 0 R/E(Physical Activity \205 Regular movement improves insulin sensitivity and gut hormone balance.)/Pg 38 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

1248 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1924 0 R/E(Meal Timing Consistency \205 Irregular eating patterns can destabilize hunger cues.)/Pg 38 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

1249 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 1925 0 R/E(Eating Pace \205 Slower eating allows satiety hormones time to activate effectively.)/Pg 38 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

1250 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 103 0 R/E(When nutrition and lifestyle work together, satiety signaling becomes clearer, more reliable, and easier to sustain long term.)/Pg 38 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

1251 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 103 0 R/E(GLP-1 thrives in conditions of balance. When the body is well-rested, regularly active, and not chronically stressed, satiety signals become clearer and blood sugar regulation improves. Small daily habits, practiced consistently, often have a greater long-term impact than extreme dietary changes.)/Pg 38 0 R/K[10]>>
