<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 105 0 R/Pg 40 0 R/A[1544 0 R 0]/K[19]>>
endobj

1307 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 106 0 R/T(Q: What is GLP-1 and why does it matter?)/E(Q: What is GLP-1 and why does it matter?)/Pg 41 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

1308 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 106 0 R/E(GLP-1 is a gut hormone involved in appetite regulation, blood sugar control, and satiety signaling. It is released after eating and helps slow digestion, stimulate insulin appropriately, and promote fullness.)/Pg 41 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

1309 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 106 0 R/T(Q: Can I naturally support GLP-1 without medication?)/E(Q: Can I naturally support GLP-1 without medication?)/Pg 41 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

1310 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 106 0 R/E(Yes. Protein intake, fiber-rich foods, balanced meal structure, consistent sleep, and regular movement all influence GLP-1 release. While medications mimic this hormone, lifestyle habits can strengthen the body\220s own signaling system.)/Pg 41 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

1311 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 106 0 R/T(Q: Do I need to count calories to support GLP-1?)/E(Q: Do I need to count calories to support GLP-1?)/Pg 41 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

1312 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 106 0 R/E(Not necessarily. Focusing on protein-forward meals, adequate fiber, and steady meal timing often improves satiety naturally and supports blood sugar stability. When fullness cues are properly supported, appetite regulation becomes more intuitive and easier to maintain.)/Pg 41 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

1313 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 106 0 R/T(Q: Are carbohydrates bad for GLP-1?)/E(Q: Are carbohydrates bad for GLP-1?)/Pg 41 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

1314 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 106 0 R/E(Carbohydrates are not inherently problematic. Whole-food, fiber-rich carbohydrates such as beans, quinoa, oats, and vegetables support stable blood sugar. Highly refined and rapidly absorbed carbohydrates are more likely to disrupt satiety signaling.)/Pg 41 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

1315 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 106 0 R/T(Q: How quickly will I notice changes?)/E(Q: How quickly will I notice changes?)/Pg 41 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

1316 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 106 0 R/E(Improvements in energy stability and hunger patterns may occur within weeks when habits are consistent and well-structured over time. Sustainable metabolic support develops gradually through steady, intentional daily practices and long-term routine consistency.)/Pg 41 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

1317 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 106 0 R/T(Q: Can stress or poor sleep affect GLP-1 function?)/E(Q: Can stress or poor sleep affect GLP-1 function?)/Pg 41 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

1318 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 106 0 R/E(Yes. Inadequate sleep and chronic stress can disrupt appetite hormones and weaken satiety signaling. Prioritizing recovery, stress management, and consistent routines supports clearer hunger cues and more stable metabolic regulation.)/Pg 41 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

1319 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 106 0 R/E(YOUR )/Pg 41 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

